<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="s_subspaces_II" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Subspaces: further examples</title>
<subsection xml:id="ss_subspace_matrices">
  <title>Important subspaces of <m>\ML_{n,n}(F)</m></title>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="s_invertibility_theorem" text="title"/> we met three families of square matrices: namely, the diagonal, upper triangular, and lower triangular matrices. (See <xref ref="d_diagonal_triangular"/>). We now introduce three more naturally occurring families. Before doing so, we give an official definition of the trace function. 
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="d_trace">
  <title>Trace of a matrix</title>
    <idx><h>trace of a matrix</h></idx>
    <notation>
      <usage><m>\tr A</m></usage>
      <description>the trace of <m>A</m></description>
    </notation>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>. The <term>trace</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted <m>\tr A</m> is defined as the sum of the diagonal entries of <m>A</m>: <ie />,
        <md>
          \tr A=\sum_{i=1}^n [A]_{i,i}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <definition xml:id="d_tracezero_symmetric_skewsymmetric">
  <title>Trace-zero, symmetric, and skew-symmetric</title>
    <idx><h>trace-zero matrix</h></idx>
    <idx><h>symmetric matrix</h></idx>
    <idx><h>skew-symmetric matrix</h></idx>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Fix an integer <m>n\geq 1</m>.
      
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            A matrix <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m> is said to be a <term>trace-zero</term> matrix if <m>\tr A=0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A matrix <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m> is <term>symmetric</term> if <m>A^T=A</m>: equivalently, if <m>[A]_{ij}=[A]_{ji}</m> for all <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A matrix <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m> is <term>skew-symmetric</term> if <m>A^T=-A</m>: equivalently, if <m>[A]_{ij}=-[A]_{ji}</m> for all <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <title>Trace-zero symmetric, and skew-symmetric <m>2\times 2</m> matrices</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The set <m>W_1</m> of all trace-zero <m>2\times 2</m> matrices can be described as
        <md>
          W_1=\left\{ \begin{amatrix}[rr]a\amp b\\ c\amp -a  \end{amatrix}\colon a,b,c\in F\right\}
        </md>.
        The set <m>W_2</m> of all symmetric <m>2\times 2</m> matrices can be described as
        <md>
          W_2=\left\{ \begin{amatrix}[rr]a\amp b\\ b\amp c  \end{amatrix}\colon a,b,c\in F\right\}
        </md>.
        The set <m>W_3</m> of all skew-symmetric <m>2\times 2</m> matrices can be described as
        <md>
          W_3=\left\{ \begin{amatrix}[rr]0\amp a\\ -a\amp 0  \end{amatrix}\colon a,b\in F\right\}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
 <remark xml:id="rm_skew-symmetric">
    <p>
      Assume <m>A</m> is a skew-symmetric <m>n\times n</m> matrix. By definition, for all <m>1\leq i\leq n</m> we must have <m>[A]_{i,i}=-[A]_{i,i}</m>, or equivalently, <m>2[A]_{i,i}=0</m>. If <m>2\ne 0</m> in our field <m>F</m>,  it follows that <m>[A]_{i,i}=0</m> for all <m>1\leq i\leq n</m>. Thus, in any field where <m>2\ne 0</m>, the diagonal entries of a skew-symmetric matrix are all equal to zero. 
    </p>
  </remark>
  <p>
    It will come as no surprise that all of the aforementioned matrix families are in fact subspaces of <m>M_{n,n}(F)</m>.
  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="th_matrix_subspaces">
    <title>Matrix subspaces</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
      Fix an integer <m>n\geq 1</m>.  Each of the following subsets of <m>M_{n,n}(F)</m> is a subspace.
  
      <ol>
        <li>
          <title>Diagonal matrices</title>
          <p>
            <m>W=\{A\in M_{n,n}(F)\colon A\text{ is diagonal}\}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Upper triangular matrices</title>
          <p>
            <m>W=\{A\in M_{n,n}(F)\colon A\text{ is upper triangular}\}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Lower triangular matrices</title>
          <p>
            <m>W=\{A\in M_{n,n}(F)\colon A\text{ is lower triangular}\}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Trace-zero matrix</title>
          <p>
            <m>W=\{A\in M_{n,n}(F)\colon \tr A=\boldzero \}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Symmetric matrices</title>
          <p>
            <m>W=\{A\in M_{n,n}(F)\colon A^T=A \}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Skew-symmetric matrices</title>
          <p>
            <m>W=\{A\in M_{n,n}(F)\colon A^T=-A \}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
       We will prove only that the set of all skew-symmetric matrices is a subspace. The other proofs are similar. Observe, that once we know that the sets of all upper triangular and lower triangular <m>n\times n</m> matrices are both subspaces, it follows from <xref ref="th_subspace_intersection"/> that the set of all diagonal matrices is a subspace, as this is precisely the intersection of the former two subspaces.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>W=\{A\in\ML_{n,n}(F)\mid A^T=-A\}</m>. We will use the 2-step technique to show <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>\ML_{n,n}(F)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, note that <m>\boldzero=\boldzero_{n\times n}</m>, the <m>n\times n</m> zero matrix, satisfies <m>\boldzero_{n\times n}^T=\boldzero_{n\times n}=-\boldzero_{n\times n}</m>. Thus <m>\boldzero\in W</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, assume <m>A,B\in W</m>, so that <m>A^T=-A</m> and <m>B^T=-B</m>. For any <m>c,d\in F</m>, we have 
        <md>
          <mrow>(cA+dB)^T \amp =cAT^+dB^T \amp (\text{transpose props.})</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =c(-A)+d(-B) \amp (A,B\in W)</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp =-(cA+dB)</mrow>
        </md>,
        showing that <m>cA+dB\in W</m>, as desired. 

      </p>
    </proof>

  </theorem>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ss_formal_polys_powerseries">
<title>Power series and formal polynomials</title>
<p>
  Our next example involves a little notational slight of hand. Namely, we will introduce the vector space of power series <m>F[[x]]</m>, as well as the subspace <m>F[x]</m> of <em>formal polynomials</em> when in essence <m>F[[x]]</m> is just the set of <m>F^\N</m> of infinite <m>F</m>-valued sequences indexed by <m>\N</m>, and <m>F[x]</m> is really just the subspace of <m>F^\N</m> consisting of sequences whose entries are zero for all but finally many entries. 
</p>
<definition xml:id="d_power_series">
  <title>Power series</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>F</m> be a field. The <term>ring of power series with coefficients in <m>F</m> in the indeterminate <m>x</m></term> is the set <m>F[[x]]</m> consisting of all infinite <m>F</m>-valued sequences <m>(a_n)_{n=0}^\infty</m>. In this context, however, a sequence <m>(a_n)_{n=0}^\infty</m> will be written as 
      <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_power_series">
        a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+\cdots =\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_kx^k
      </md>.
      Given a power series <m>\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_kx_k</m>, we call <m>a_kx_k</m> the <term><m>k</m>-th term</term> of the series, and <m>a_k</m> its <term><m>k</m>-th coefficient</term>.
      We will use function-like notation to denote elements of <m>F[[x]]</m>, favoring letters like <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, and <m>h</m>, and writing <m>f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_kx^k </m> when explicitly introducing the coefficients of <m>f</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Since <m>F[[x]]</m> is equal to <m>F^\N</m> but with a different choice of notation, it inherits the notion of equality of <m>F^\N</m>, as well as its vector space structure. That is given any power series <m>f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_kx^k</m> and <m>g(x)=\sum_{k=0}^\infty b_kx^k</m> and any scalar <m>c\in F</m>, we have 
      <md>
        <mrow>f=g \amp \iff a_k=b_k \text{ for all } k\in \N</mrow>
        <mrow>(f+g)(x) \amp =\sum_{k=0}^\infty (a_k+b_k)x^k</mrow>
        <mrow>(cf)(x) \amp =\sum_{k=0}^\infty ca_k x^k</mrow>
      </md>.
      As an additional layer of algebraic structure, we define the <term>product</term> of two power series <m>f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_kx^k</m> and <m>g(x)=\sum_{k=0}^\infty b_kx^k</m> as follows:
      <md number="yes">
        <mrow>(fg)(x) \amp =\sum_{k=0}^\infty c_kx^k \amp c_k=\sum_{j=0}^ka_jb_{k-j} \text{ for all } k\in \N</mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
<remark>
<title>Power series multiplication</title>
<p>
Power series multiplication, that extra layer of structure mentioned above, can be thought of as the motivation for using our notation <m>f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_kx^k</m>, as opposed to the usual tuple notation <m>(a_k)_{k\in \N}</m>. For one thing, identifying <m>x^n</m> as the power series with a 1 for the <m>n</m>-th coefficient and a zero elsewhere, it is easy to see that the power series multiplication rule implies <m>x^n\cdot x^m=x^{n+m}</m> for all <m>n,m\in \N</m>. General power series multiplication can then be understood as a sort of infinite <q>foiling</q> process, where we expand a product out via distribution, collect like terms, and then distribute again: 
<md>
  <mrow>fg\amp =(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+\cdots )(b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2+\cdots)</mrow>
  <mrow> \amp = a_0b_0+(a_0b_1+a_1b_0)x+(a_0b_2+a_1b_1+a_2b_0)x^2+\cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</remark>
<remark>
<title>Properties of power series multiplication</title>
<p>
It is easy to prove that power series multiplication is commutative, associative, and distributive. In more detail, for any <m>f,g,h\in F[[x]]</m>, we have 
<md>
  <mrow>fg \amp =gf \amp f(gh)\amp=(fg)h
  \amp f(g+h) \amp =(g+h)f =fg+fh</mrow>
</md>.
Furthermore, we identify <m>F</m> as a subset of <m>F[[x]]</m> by identifying a scalar <m>c\in F</m> with the power series <m>c=c+0x+0x^2+\cdots</m>. Note that we have
<md>
<mrow>(c+0x+0x^2+\cdots)+(d+0x+0x^2+\cdots) \amp = (c+d)+0x+0x^2+\cdots +c+0x+0x^2+\cdots</mrow>
  <mrow>(c+0x+0x^2+\cdots)(d+0x+0x^2+\cdots) \amp = cd+0x+0x^2+\cdots</mrow>
</md>,
showing that this identification of <m>F</m> as a subset of <m>F[[x]]</m> preserves the notions of addition and multiplication on <m>F</m>. 
</p>
</remark>
<p>
  We now define the set of formal polynomials <m>F[x]</m> as the subspace of <m>F[[x]]</m> consisting of all power series whose coefficients are equal to zero for all but finitely many indices. 
</p>
<definition xml:id="d_formal_polynomials">
  <title>Formal polynomials</title>
<statement>
   <p>
  Let <m>F</m> be a field.
  Define the <term>polynomial ring over <m>F</m> in the indeterminate <m>x</m></term> to be the subset <m>F[x]\subseteq F[[x]]</m> of all power series whose coefficients are equal to zero for all but finitely many indices: <ie/>, 
  <md>
    <mrow>F[x] \amp = \{f(x)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty a_nx^n\in F[[x]]\mid a_n=0 \text{ for all } n&gt;  N \text{ for some } N\in \Z_{\geq 0} \} </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp =\{f\in F[[x]]\mid f(x)=\sum_{n=0}^Na_nx^n \text{ for some } N\in \Z_{\geq 0}, a_n\in F\}</mrow>
  </md>.
  Restricted to <m>F[x]</m>, the power series multiplication rule becomes the familiar polynomial multiplication rule: 
<md number="yes">
    <mrow xml:id="eq_poly_mult">(\anpoly)\amp \cdot (\bmpoly) =\sum_{k=0}^{m+n}c_kx^k</mrow>
     <mrow>c_k\amp =\sum_{j=0}^k a_j b_{k-j}</mrow>
  </md>.
  As illustrated above, when denoting polynomials we will usually drop all the terms with zero coefficients: <eg/>,  <m>f(x)=a_0+a_1x+\cdots +a_nx^n</m>, or <m>f(x)=\anpoly</m>.
</p>

<p>
  Given a nonzero polynomial <m>f\in F[x]</m>, we define its <term>degree</term>, <m>\deg f</m>, to be the largest <m>n</m> satisfying <m>a_n\ne 0</m>.
  If <m>f = \boldzero</m>, we define <m>\deg f=-\infty</m>.
</p>

<p>
  If <m>f(x)=\anpoly</m> is a polynomial of degree <m>n\ne -\infty</m>, we call <m>a_{n}</m> the <term>leading coefficient</term> of <m>f</m>.
  A polynomial with leading coefficient equal to <m>1\in R</m> is called <term>monic</term>.
</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <definition xml:id="d_poly_bounded_n">
  <title>Polynomials of bounded degree</title>
  <statement>
  <p>
  Let <m>F</m> be a field. Given <m>n\in \N</m>, we define <m>F[x]_{\deg \leq n}</m> to be the set of polynomials of <m>F[x]</m> of degree at most <m>n</m>: <ie/>,
  <md>
    F[x]_{\deg \leq n}=\{f\in F[x]\mid \deg f\leq n\}.
  </md>
  Note that the zero polynomial <m>\boldzero</m> is understood to be an element of <m>F[x]_{\deg\leq n}</m> based on the convention that <m>\deg \boldzero=-\infty&lt; n</m> for all <m>n\in \N</m>. 
  </p>
  </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Not surprisingly, the nested sets <m>F[x]_{\deg\leq n}\subseteq F[x]\subseteq F[[x]]</m> are all subspaces of one another.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop_poly_subspaces">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>F</m> be a field and let <m>n\in \N</m>. The inclusions 
        <md>
          F[x]_{\deg\leq n}\subseteq F[x]\subseteq F[[x]]
        </md>
        are subspace inclusions: <ie/>, in the chain above, each set is a subspace of the next. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof is left to the reader. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Besides being used to define an interesting subspace of <m>F[x]</m> and <m>F[[x]]</m>, the degree function will prove to be important to us in many ways, as the next results begin to show. 
  </p>

    <p>
        The degree operation on <m>F[x]</m> turns out to be very useful, thanks in part to the properties articulated in the following proposition.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop_deg_poly">
        <title>Polynomial degree properties</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>F</m> be a field, and let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be nonzero polynomials in <m>F[x]</m>.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\deg fg=\deg f+\deg g</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\deg(f+g)\leq \max\{\deg f, \deg g\}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                        Assume <m>\deg f=n</m> and <m>\deg g=m</m>, so that 
                <md>
                    <mrow>f(x) \amp =\anpoly \amp </mrow>
                    <mrow>g(x)\amp=\bmpoly \amp </mrow>
                </md>,
                where <m>a_n\ne 0</m> and <m>b_m\ne 0</m>. From the polynomial multiplication rule given in <xref ref="eq_poly_mult"/>, we see that 
                <md>
                    <mrow>fg(x) \amp =a_nb_mx^{n+m}+\text{terms of lower degree}</mrow>
                </md>.
                It follows that <m>\deg fg\leq n+m=\deg f+\deg g</m>. If <m>F</m> is an integral domain, then <m>a_nb_m\ne 0</m> and thus <m>\deg fg=m+n=\deg f+\deg g</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The proof is left to the reader. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </proof>
    </proposition>
    <theorem xml:id="th_division_alg_poly">
      <title>Division algorithm for polynomials</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>F</m> be a field, let <m>f,g\in F[x]</m>, and suppose <m>g\ne \boldzero</m>. There is a unique pair of polynomials <m>(q,r)\in F[x]\times F[x]</m> satisfying the following conditions. 
          <ol marker="(i)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f=qg+r</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>r=\boldzero</m> or <m>0\leq \deg r&lt; \deg g</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The proof is left as a homework exercise. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <definition xml:id="d_poly_roots">
    <title>Roots of polynomials</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    Let <m>F</m> be a field. Given a polynomial <m>f(x)=\anpoly\in F[x]</m> and element <m>r\in F</m>, we define 
    <md>
      f(r)=a_nr^n+a_{n-1}r^{n-1}+\cdots a_1r+a_0\in F
    </md>.
    An element <m>r\in F</m> is a <term>root</term> of <m>f</m> if <m>f(r)=0</m>. 
    </p>
    </statement>
    </definition>
    
    <corollary xml:id="cor_poly_distinct_roots">
    <title>Distinct roots of polynomials</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>F</m> be a field, and let <m>f\in F[x]</m>. 
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                For all <m>r\in F</m>, we have <m>f(r)=0</m> if and only if <m>f=(x-r)g(x)</m> for some polynomial <m>g\in F[x]</m>. 
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Assume <m>f\ne \boldzero</m>. If <m>\deg f=n</m>, then <m>f</m> has at most <m>n</m> distinct roots. 
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
              Assume <m>n\in \N</m>, and that <m>f,g\in F[x]</m> satisfy <m>\deg f=\deg g=n</m>. If there are <m>n+1</m> distinct elements <m>r_1,r_2,\dots, r_{n+1}\in F</m> such that <m>f(r_i)=g(r_i)</m>, then <m>f=g</m>. 
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The proof is left as a homework exercise. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ss_function_spaces">
<title>Function spaces</title>
<definition xml:id="d_polynomials">
<title>Polynomial functions</title>
<statement>
<p>
  Let <m>F</m> be a field, and let <m>I</m> be a nonempty subset of <m>F</m>. A function <m>f\in \mathcal{F}(I,F)</m> is a <term>polynomial function</term> if there is a formal polynomial <m>g\in F[x]</m> such that <m>f(x)=g(x)</m> for all <m>x\in I</m>. We say that the polynomial <m>g</m> <term>represents</term> <m>f</m> in this case.
</p>
<p>
  We define <m>P(I)</m> to be the set of all polynomial functions on <m>I</m>, and we define <m>P_n(I)</m> to the set of all functions <m>f\in \mathcal{F}(I,F)</m> that can be represented by a polynomial of degree at most <m>n</m>: <ie/>, 
  <md>
    <mrow>P(I) \amp =\{f\in \mathcal{F}(I,F)\mid f(x)=g(x) \text{ for all } x\in I, \text{ for some } g\in F[x]\}</mrow>
    <mrow>P_n(I) \amp =\{f\in \mathcal{F}(I,F)\mid f(x)=g(x) \text{ for all } x\in I, \text{ for some } g\in F[x]_{\deg\leq n}\}</mrow>
  </md>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>
<remark>
<title>Formal polynomials and polynomial functions</title>
<p>
There is a subtle issue lurking behind this polynomial function business: namely, it is possible that a polynomial function <m>f\in P(I)</m> can be represented by more than one formal polynomial <m>g\in F[x]</m>. Indeed, consider the case where the subset <m>I\subseteq F</m> is the singleton <m>I=\{1\}</m>. In this case, any function <m>f\in \mathcal{F}(I,F)</m> is completely determined by its value <m>f(1)=c</m> at the one element of the domain <m>I</m>. As a result, it is possible to give many different polynomial representation of a single function <m>f</m>. For example, the zero function <m>0_I</m> can be represented by the zero polynomial <m>\boldzero</m>, but also by <m>f(x)=(x-1)^n</m> for any <m>n\in \Z_+</m>. We see then, that we do not always have a 1-1 correspondence between elements of <m>F[x]</m> and <m>P(I)</m>. We will be able to give a much more precise analysis of the relationship between these two spaces once we introduce linear transformations. 
</p>
</remark>

<proposition xml:id="prop_polynomial_space">
<title>Polynomial spaces</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>I</m> be a nonempty subset of the field <m>F</m>. 
<ol>
  <li>
    <p>
      <m>P(I)</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathcal{F}(I,F)</m>. 
    </p>
  </li>
   <li>
     <p>
     <m>P_n(I)</m> is a subspace of <m>\mathcal{F}(I,F)</m> for all <m>n\in \N</m>. 
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>
  We prove that <m>P_n(I)</m> is a subspace, using the 2-step technique. The proof for <m>P(I)</m> is similar. 
  <ol marker="(i)">
    <li>
      <p>
        Recall that the zero vector of <m>\mathcal{F}(I,F)</m> is the the zero function <m>O_I\colon I\rightarrow F</m>, defined as <m>0_I(x)=0</m> for all <m>x\in I</m>. Writing 
        <md>
          0_I(x)=0x^n+0x^{n-1}+\cdots +0x+0
        </md>,
        we see that <m>\boldzero=0_I\in P_n(I)</m>. (More generally, any constant function <m>f(x)=c</m> is an element of <m>P_n(I)</m> for all <m>n\geq 0</m>.)
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Assume <m>f,g\in P_n(I)</m>. By definition this means that there are constants <m>a_i, b_i\in F</m>, <m>1\leq i\leq n</m>, such that 
        <md>
          <mrow>f(x) \amp = \anpoly</mrow>
          <mrow>g(x) \amp = b_nx^n+b_{n-1}x^{n-1}+\cdots+b_1x+b_0</mrow>
        </md>
        for all <m>x\in I</m>. It follows that 
        <md>
          <mrow>(cf+dg)(x) \amp = (a_n+b_n)x^n+(a_{n-1}+b_{n-1})x^{n-1}+\cdots+(a_1+b_1)x+(a_0+b_0) </mrow>
        </md>
        for all <m>x\in I</m>, showing that <m>cf+dg\in P_n(I)</m>, as desired. 
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>
</proof>
</proposition>


